{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify what you're rounding to (d.p. or s.f.) and locate the relevant digit",
      "Look at the next digit: 5+ rounds up, 4 or less stays the same",
      "For estimation: round every number to 1 s.f. first, then calculate",
      "For decimal arithmetic: line up decimal points for +/−; count places for ×"
    ],
    "title": "How to Round Numbers and Estimate Calculations",
    "content": "<p><strong>Rounding to decimal places (d.p.):</strong> Count that many digits after the decimal point, then look at the next digit. If it's 5 or more, round up; if it's 4 or less, round down (keep it the same).</p><p><strong>Rounding to significant figures (s.f.):</strong> The first significant figure is the first non-zero digit. Count that many significant figures from the first non-zero digit, then round using the same 5-or-more rule. For large numbers, fill remaining places with zeros. For small decimals (like 0.00347), the leading zeros are not significant.</p><p><strong>Estimation:</strong> Round every number in a calculation to 1 significant figure, then work out the simplified version. This gives a quick approximate answer to check your work or to answer an 'estimate' question on the exam. Show your rounded values clearly.</p><p><strong>Decimal arithmetic:</strong> For addition and subtraction, line up the decimal points. For multiplication, ignore the decimals, multiply as whole numbers, then count the total decimal places in both numbers and put the point back. For division, make the divisor a whole number by multiplying both numbers by 10, 100, etc.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Estimate</strong> \\(\\frac{49.3 \\times 0.208}{0.97}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1 — Round each to 1 s.f.:</strong> \\(49.3 \\approx 50\\), \\(0.208 \\approx 0.2\\), \\(0.97 \\approx 1\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Multiply:</strong> \\(50 \\times 0.2 = 10\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Divide:</strong> \\(10 \\div 1 = 10\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(\\approx 10\\)</p>"
  },
